&nbsp;

The Representative of the Earth is part of the Celestial Tribunal.

If the "Game Over" condition happens in eg. Rook City, the Representative of the Earth card&nbsp;doesn't belong to the Rook City Environment. So the card goes back to the Celestial Tribunal trash. Because the Rook City cards are all removed from the game, and Representative of the Earth isn't a Rook City Card.

</blockquote>
</div>

&nbsp;

I think you've misunderstood Matchickman's point. He's saying that regardless of where the Representative of Earth is when it's destroyed, destroying it is a "game over" condition for the Celestial Tribunal, not for any other environment, so it should be the Celestial Tribunal that is removed from the game, not the environment the Represeantative happens to be in.

[/quote]

I believe this might actually go back to the "cards cannot affect things in another battle zone" issue. Regardless of what deck the card is from, the card has a game over condition and is&nbsp;<em>in a specific battle zone</em>. It can't declare a Game Over condition (thus removing an environment) in the other battle zone, as it doesn't explicitly mention another battle zone.
